course: dictionary
lesson: 5590
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Liberty has more than one meaning, so context decides which one fits. Its main meaning is freedom: the right to live, speak and act as you choose without being controlled or locked up unfairly. In «Millions of immigrants came to America, many passing the Statue of Liberty», the name is about the freedom the statue stands for.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «The young country spread west to the Pacific, taking land from Native nations, and welcomed millions of immigrants who sailed past the Statue of Liberty.» The statue is a famous monument, so liberty here is a name for a symbol of freedom, not a cheeky act.
explain: Other meanings are in everyday and formal use. «I am not at liberty to say» means I am not allowed to say; it is formal. «What a liberty!» means someone has been cheeky or rude. «I took the liberty of booking a table» means I did it without asking first. Test each meaning by swapping it into the sentence and checking that it makes sense.
fact: Liberty means freedom: the right to live, speak and act as you choose.
fact: «At liberty» means free, not in prison, or allowed to do something in formal language.
fact: A «liberty» is a cheeky or rude act, and «taking a liberty» means doing something without permission.
fact: «The Statue of Liberty» is a giant statue holding up a torch, a gift from France, in New York.
fact: The Statue of Liberty is made of copper sheets, and it turned green as a protective coat formed on it.
exercise: What does liberty mean? | A cheeky or rude act | *Freedom to live, speak and act as you choose | A gift from France | A green coat that protects copper
exercise: «I took the liberty of booking a table.» What does took the liberty mean here? | Paid for the table with money | Booked it for a very long time | *Booked it without asking first | Forgot to book it at all
exercise: «I am not at liberty to say.» What does at liberty mean here? | *Not allowed to say | Free to say anything at all | Very loud and clear | Kind and helpful to everyone
test: «What a liberty!» is shouted when someone takes your seat on the bus. What does liberty mean here? | A gift from France | A statue of a seat | Freedom to sit wherever you like | *Cheeky or rude behaviour
test: «The Statue of Liberty, a giant statue holding up a torch» tells you that the statue is what? | A kind of cheeky act | *A giant statue holding a torch, a gift from France | A type of copper coin | A large ship that sails to Europe
exam: In «He took liberties with the rules», what does liberties mean? | A green coat of copper | *Cheeky acts done without permission | Long, kind holidays | Freedoms to act as he chose
